  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1149 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nDense Fog...another night with dense fog to contend with as south\nto southeast flow over Lake Michigan has once again allowed dense\nfog to develop across far eastern WI near Lake Michigan this \nevening. Mid-clouds increasing from the west through the night \nshould help confine the fog to far eastern WI near Lake Michigan \ninto Saturday morning, with a little westward and northward \nexpansion possible, especially near the Bay. The fog will mix out\nand lift by mid-Saturday morning as winds and clouds increase. \nWill run the Dense Fog Advisory for the lakeshore counties \nthrough 9AM.\n\nRain Chances...weak push of WAA and ribbon of moisture could \nproduce a few sprinkles across central and northern WI during the \npre-dawn hours of Saturday. Then, as a shortwave and better \nmoisture arrives, along with a frontal boundary sagging into the \narea, showers chances increase to 30-50% after dark on Saturday \ninto Sunday morning. While PWATs climb to ~1.25\", overall forcing \nis lacking so not expecting much rain with probabilities of over \na quarter inch under 5%. The front will shift just to our south \nSunday afternoon into Monday, then lift back north early next \nweek. Better dynamics will reside to our north/west, but parts of \ncentral and north-central WI will see chances for rain (30-70%) \nlate Monday into Tuesday as at least a couple shortwaves and \npushes of WAA cross the northern Plains/western Great Lakes and \ninteract with the boundary. Another shot at rain arrives on \nWednesday as a potentially more potent shortwave and frontal \nboundary cross the region.\n\nTemperatures...the weekend will start off on the mild side with\nwell above normal temps continuing as southerly flow persists.\nHighs in the upper 60s to mid 70s are forecast, cooler near the\nshores of Lake Michigan. As the frontal boundary sinks south,\nhighs on Sunday will fall into the 50s in north-central WI, but\nremain in the upper 60s to around 70 in east-central WI. The\nentire area will see temps return closer to seasonal norms on \nMonday and Tuesday with highs in the 40s and 50s. The cooler temps\nwill be short-lived as return flow sets up on Wednesday and \nreadings rebound back above normal.",
